49: % ======================================================================
50: \section{Overview of the Sweep Campaign}
51: \label{sec:overview}
52: % ======================================================================
53: 
54: This manual provides a detailed description of the Python campaign suite developed to investigate the physical properties of a 2D compass-needle array. The central scientific question addressed by this infrastructure is:
55: \begin{quote}
56: \textit{``How does the viscous damping coefficient control the shape of the hysteresis loop and the scaling statistics of magnetic avalanches in classical dipolar lattices, and how does this behavior depend on the underlying geometry (square, triangular, or honeycomb)?''}
57: \end{quote}
58: 
59: The campaign consists of three main components:
60: \begin{enumerate}
61:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweep.py}}: Runs the simulation campaign sequentially on a single CPU core.
62:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweepV02.py}}: Parallelizes the execution of the sweep runs across multiple CPU cores, using worker sandboxing to prevent file collisions and providing resume capabilities.
63:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}}: Processes the generated raw campaign logs, calculates hysteresis metrics, detects avalanche events, performs power-law fits via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE), and renders final publication-quality figures.
64: \end{enumerate}
65: 
66: \medskip
67: The sweep execution proceeds in two distinct stages for each configuration:
68: \begin{itemize}
69:     \item \textbf{Stage 1: Hysteresis}: Sweeps the external magnetic field through a five-ramp cycle ($0 \to +B_{\max} \to 0 \to -B_{\max} \to 0 \to +B_{\max}$) to trace the magnetization curve ($M \times B$) and capture avalanches.
70:     \item \textbf{Stage 2: Relaxation}: Sets the external field to zero and lets the system relax freely from its initial random state to analyze domain sizes and domain-count statistics.
71: \end{itemize}
72: 
73: % ======================================================================
74: \section{The Damping Grid and Quality Factor}
75: \label{sec:damping_grid}
76: % ======================================================================
77: 
78: Rather than sweeping damping coefficients arbitrarily, the campaign builds a log-spaced grid in terms of the physical \textbf{Quality Factor} ($Q$), defined as:
79: \begin{equation}
80: Q = \frac{\omega_0 I}{b}
81: \end{equation}
82: where:
83: \begin{itemize}
84:     \item $I$ is the moment of inertia of each needle [kg$\cdot$m$^2$].
85:     \item $b$ is the viscous damping coefficient [N$\cdot$m$\cdot$s/rad].
86:     \item $\omega_0$ is the natural angular frequency of a needle under the dipolar field of its nearest neighbor at center-to-center distance $r_{\mathrm{nn}}$:
87:     \begin{equation}
88:     \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{m B_{\mathrm{ref}}}{I}}
89:     \end{equation}
90:     where $m$ is the needle magnetic moment [A$\cdot$m$^2$], and $B_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the nearest-neighbor dipolar coupling field:
91:     \begin{equation}
92:     B_{\mathrm{ref}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{2m}{r_{\mathrm{nn}}^3}
93:     \end{equation}
94: \end{itemize}
95: 
96: By defaults, the grid covers the range $Q \in [0.05, 15.0]$, spanning three distinct dynamical regimes:
97: \begin{enumerate}
98:     \item \textbf{Underdamped} ($Q \gg 1$, e.g., $Q = 15.0$): Needles visibly oscillate before aligning.
99:     \item \textbf{Crossover / Critically Damped} ($Q \sim 1$, e.g., $Q = 0.866$): Fast relaxation with minimal oscillation.
100:     \item \textbf{Overdamped} ($Q \ll 1$, e.g., $Q = 0.05$): Slow, monotonic relaxation without oscillations.
