77: \section{Physics \& Theoretical Model}
78: \label{sec:physics}
79: % ======================================================================
80: 
81: The \code{compass\_sim} simulator resolves the real Newtonian rotational equations of motion for a two-dimensional lattice of classical magnetic dipoles (compass needles). For each needle $i$, the dynamics are governed by:
82: 
83: \begin{equation}
84: I \ddot{\theta}_i = \tau_i^{\text{dip}} + \tau_i^{\text{ext}} - b \dot{\theta}_i + \tau_i^{\text{noise}}
85: \label{eq:motion}
86: \end{equation}
87: 
88: \noindent
89: where the parameters are defined as follows:
90: \begin{itemize}
91:     \item $\theta_i$ is the in-plane angular orientation of the $i$-th needle relative to the $+x$ axis.
92:     \item $I$ is the needle's physical moment of inertia (\si{kg.m^2}).
93:     \item $\tau_i^{\text{dip}}$ is the mechanical torque exerted on needle $i$ by the total dipolar magnetic field $\vec{B}_i^{\text{dip}}$ generated by all other needles in the lattice.
94:     \item $\tau_i^{\text{ext}} = m (\hat{u}_i \times \vec{B}^{\text{ext}})_z$ is the torque exerted by the uniform external magnetic field $\vec{B}^{\text{ext}}$, where $m$ is the magnetic moment of the needle and $\hat{u}_i = (\cos\theta_i, \sin\theta_i)$ is its orientation vector.
95:     \item $b$ is the viscous air damping coefficient (\si{N.m.s/rad}), which governs dissipation.
96:     \item $\tau_i^{\text{noise}}$ is a random thermal noise torque, active only during simulated annealing runs.
97: \end{itemize}
98: 
99: The dipolar magnetic field $\vec{B}_i^{\text{dip}}$ generated at the center of needle $i$ by all other needles $j \neq i$ is computed using the full anisotropic dipolar formula:
100: 
101: \begin{equation}
102: \vec{B}_i^{\text{dip}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \sum_{j \neq i} \frac{3(\vec{m}_j \cdot \hat{r}_{ij})\hat{r}_{ij} - \vec{m}_j}{r_{ij}^3}
103: \end{equation}
104: 
105: \noindent
106: where $\vec{r}_{ij} = \vec{r}_i - \vec{r}_j$, $r_{ij} = |\vec{r}_{ij}|$, and $\hat{r}_{ij} = \vec{r}_{ij}/r_{ij}$.
