201: \section{Research and publication strategy}
202: \label{sec:research}
203: % ======================================================================
204: 
205: \subsection{The gap in the literature}
206: 
207: \noindent The review covered three fronts. \textbf{(i)}~Classical compass-needle arrays: the founding work (square $22\times22$ lattice, Phys.\ Rev.\ B \textbf{58}, 9238, 1998~\cite{prb1998}) uses equilibrium Monte Carlo with a random field mimicking thermal noise, not Newtonian dynamics. \textbf{(ii)}~Classical dipoles in honeycomb/kagome/triangular lattices (2012--2025): Luttinger--Tisza methods, energy minimization, spin waves, and Monte Carlo with \emph{parallel tempering}, all focused on ground states and thermal \emph{equilibrium} transitions. \textbf{(iii)}~\emph{Artificial spin ice}~\cite{nisoli2013}: real temporal dynamics is studied, but through discrete macrospins (Ising-like) or experimental data (MOKE/MFM), almost never with continuous angular dynamics and explicit mechanical inertia.
208: 
209: The question of how damping controls hysteresis and avalanches is active and publishable in adjacent fields. It is established that underdamped and overdamped systems belong to different universality classes in short-range or mean-field systems~\cite{dahmen2011}, but this has not been answered for complete dipolar coupling (long-range, anisotropic) with real spatial geometry. This is the gap occupied by the project.
210: 
211: \subsection{Study design and decision criterion}
212: 
213: The central study sweeps damping over $\sim 2.5$ orders of magnitude in $Q$ (from $Q \approx 15$, underdamped, to $Q \approx 0.05$, overdamped), with multiple initial-noise seeds in the three geometries. It measures hysteresis-loop area, coercive field, remanence, avalanche statistics (power-law exponent $\tau$ in $P(s) \sim s^{-\tau}$, fitted by maximum likelihood using the Clauset--Shalizi--Newman method~\cite{clauset2009}), and domain-size distribution in free relaxation.
214: 
215: The decision criterion was defined \emph{a priori}:
216: \begin{itemize}
217: \item \textbf{Result A (incremental, solid)}: $\tau$ approximately constant with $Q$ (only scales/cutoffs change), consistent with known phenomenology and publishable in the standard target journals;
218: \item \textbf{Result B (surprising)}: $\tau$ varies systematically with $Q$, or there is a qualitative difference between geometries, signaling a breakdown of universality due to anisotropic long-range dipolar coupling. This would justify attempting PRL or a similar venue.
219: \end{itemize}
220: 
221: \subsection{Target journals and APC funding}
222: 
223: \begin{itemize}
224: \item \textbf{CAPES--ACS agreement}: it was confirmed that the CAPES transformative \emph{Read \& Publish} agreement covers \textbf{100\% of the APC in any ACS journal} (CC~BY license) for corresponding authors from consortium institutions, with eligibility checked during submission via ORCID, removing cost as a decision factor;
225: \item \textbf{Primary target}: \emph{ACS Materials Au} (IF~6.5, Q2), the highest-impact fully \emph{open access} ACS option. The paper must be framed as a model for \emph{artificial spin ice} and patterned nanomagnets, which was incorporated into the introduction design;
226: \item \textbf{Backup target}: \emph{ACS Physical Chemistry Au} (IF~4.3, Q2), whose scope explicitly includes condensed matter and is safer in fit;
227: \item \textbf{High-impact alternatives}: \emph{Physical Review Letters} is \emph{outside} the CAPES agreement (APS is not a participating publisher); \emph{Nature Communications}, being \emph{gold OA}, is excluded from \emph{Read \& Publish} agreements (APC $\sim$ USD~6,190 separately). Both are justified only for a type-B result;
228: \item \textbf{Other CAPES publishers} (Springer Nature $\sim$1,750 hybrid titles, Elsevier $\sim$160, Wiley, IEEE, RSP, ACM): a partial review did not find, up to letter~H of the Springer list, a title with better fit than the ACS targets; natural Elsevier candidates (\emph{Physica A}, \emph{JMMM}) await verification in the eligible-title list.
229: \end{itemize}
230: 
231: % ======================================================================
232: \section{Campaign infrastructure and pilot study}
233: \label{sec:campaign}
234: % ======================================================================
235: 
236: \subsection{The two scripts}
237: 
238: \textbf{\code{damping\_sweep.py}} orchestrates the campaign by calling the simulator programmatically without modifying the source code. It builds the log-spaced damping grid in $Q$ from the real physics (inertia and moment derived from geometry), runs Step~1 (hysteresis) and Step~2 (free relaxation with domain statistics) for each geometry~$\times$~$Q$~$\times$~seed combination, and saves per-run CSVs, JSON metadata (including $Q$, $\omega_0$, $B_{\mathrm{ref}}$, domain distribution), and a consolidated \code{manifest.csv}.
239: 
240: \textbf{\code{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}} reads \code{manifest.csv} and produces: loop area (closed-line integral), coercivity and remanence (interpolation at zero crossings); avalanche detection in $dM/dB$ within each monotonic ramp segment (robust threshold of $4\times$MAD); power-law fitting by MLE with $x_{\min}$ selected by KS distance~\cite{clauset2009}, \emph{aggregating avalanches from all seeds} for each $(geometry, Q)$ point before fitting, a methodological choice required for reliable statistics; and final plots, including the central $\tau(Q)$ test by geometry.
241: 
242: Validations performed: area against an exact rectangular loop; $B_c$/$M_r$ against a synthetic loop with known values; avalanche detection against injected steps (size recovery with $\sim$1\% error); MLE against a synthetic sample with exact $\tau = 2.5$ (recovered $2.516 \pm 0.021$), through both code paths (the \code{powerlaw} package, with a real bug in the installed version corrected, and the in-house fallback implementation, with identical results).
243: 
244: \subsection{Pilot-study results}
245: 
246: A reduced pilot (array $14\times14 = 196$ needles, 5 $Q$ values, 3 seeds, 3 geometries, shortened cycle) validated the full pipeline and already displays the central signal sought (Figure~\ref{fig:tau} and Table~\ref{tab:pilot}): the exponent $\tau$ \textbf{increases systematically with $Q$} in all three geometries, from $\tau \approx 1.2$--$1.3$ in the \emph{crossover} ($Q \approx 0.87$) to $\tau \approx 1.7$--$1.8$ in the underdamped regime ($Q = 15$). For the two most overdamped $Q$ values, the shortened pilot cycle does not generate enough avalanches for fitting, which is the correct pipeline behavior: it reports unavailability instead of a spurious number.
