49: % ======================================================================
50: \section{Overview of the Sweep Campaign}
51: \label{sec:overview}
52: % ======================================================================
53: 
54: This manual provides a detailed description of the Python campaign suite developed to investigate the physical properties of a 2D compass-needle array. The central scientific question addressed by this infrastructure is:
55: \begin{quote}
56: \textit{``How does the viscous damping coefficient control the shape of the hysteresis loop and the scaling statistics of magnetic avalanches in classical dipolar lattices, and how does this behavior depend on the underlying geometry (square, triangular, or honeycomb)?''}
57: \end{quote}
58: 
59: The campaign consists of three main components:
60: \begin{enumerate}
61:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweep.py}}: Runs the simulation campaign sequentially on a single CPU core.
62:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweepV02.py}}: Parallelizes the execution of the sweep runs across multiple CPU cores, using worker sandboxing to prevent file collisions and providing resume capabilities.
63:     \item \textbf{\texttt{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}}: Processes the generated raw campaign logs, calculates hysteresis metrics, detects avalanche events, performs power-law fits via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE), and renders final publication-quality figures.
64: \end{enumerate}
65: 
66: \medskip
67: The sweep execution proceeds in two distinct stages for each configuration:
68: \begin{itemize}
69:     \item \textbf{Stage 1: Hysteresis}: Sweeps the external magnetic field through a five-ramp cycle ($0 \to +B_{\max} \to 0 \to -B_{\max} \to 0 \to +B_{\max}$) to trace the magnetization curve ($M \times B$) and capture avalanches.
70:     \item \textbf{Stage 2: Relaxation}: Sets the external field to zero and lets the system relax freely from its initial random state to analyze domain sizes and domain-count statistics.
71: \end{itemize}
72: 
73: % ======================================================================
74: \section{The Damping Grid and Quality Factor}
75: \label{sec:damping_grid}
76: % ======================================================================
77: 
78: Rather than sweeping damping coefficients arbitrarily, the campaign builds a log-spaced grid in terms of the physical \textbf{Quality Factor} ($Q$), defined as:
79: \begin{equation}
80: Q = \frac{\omega_0 I}{b}
81: \end{equation}
82: where:
83: \begin{itemize}
84:     \item $I$ is the moment of inertia of each needle [kg$\cdot$m$^2$].
85:     \item $b$ is the viscous damping coefficient [N$\cdot$m$\cdot$s/rad].
86:     \item $\omega_0$ is the natural angular frequency of a needle under the dipolar field of its nearest neighbor at center-to-center distance $r_{\mathrm{nn}}$:
87:     \begin{equation}
88:     \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{m B_{\mathrm{ref}}}{I}}
89:     \end{equation}
90:     where $m$ is the needle magnetic moment [A$\cdot$m$^2$], and $B_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the nearest-neighbor dipolar coupling field:
91:     \begin{equation}
92:     B_{\mathrm{ref}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{2m}{r_{\mathrm{nn}}^3}
93:     \end{equation}
94: \end{itemize}
95: 
96: By defaults, the grid covers the range $Q \in [0.05, 15.0]$, spanning three distinct dynamical regimes:
97: \begin{enumerate}
98:     \item \textbf{Underdamped} ($Q \gg 1$, e.g., $Q = 15.0$): Needles visibly oscillate before aligning.
99:     \item \textbf{Crossover / Critically Damped} ($Q \sim 1$, e.g., $Q = 0.866$): Fast relaxation with minimal oscillation.
100:     \item \textbf{Overdamped} ($Q \ll 1$, e.g., $Q = 0.05$): Slow, monotonic relaxation without oscillations.
101: \end{enumerate}
102: 
103: % ======================================================================
104: \section{\texttt{damping\_sweep.py} (Serial Campaign)}
105: \label{sec:damping_sweep}
106: % ======================================================================
107: 
108: The \texttt{damping\_sweep.py} script orchestrates the campaign by importing the simulator engine \texttt{compass.py} and calling its core \texttt{relax()} function programmatically for each point in the parameter sweep:
109: \begin{center}
110: \textbf{Geometry} $\times$ \textbf{Damping} ($Q$) $\times$ \textbf{Initial Seed}
111: \end{center}
112: It executes runs sequentially on a single core.
113: 
114: \subsection{Physical Parameter Consistency}
115: To ensure physical consistency, the script computes the physical properties of a reference needle using a square geometry. The same set of physical damping values $b$ [N$\cdot$m$\cdot$s/rad] is then applied to the other geometries. Because the nearest-neighbor distance $r_{\mathrm{nn}}$ changes with geometry, the effective $Q$ varies slightly across lattices; this variation is recorded in the metadata and correctly handled during analysis.
116: 
117: \subsection{Command-Line Interface}
118: The script accepts the following parameters:
119: \begin{itemize}
120:     \item \texttt{--out\_dir} (Required): Directory where results will be written.
121:     \item \texttt{--grid\_n} (Default: 30): Grid size $N \times M$ of the array (e.g. 30 $\to$ 900 needles).
122:     \item \texttt{--n\_seeds} (Default: 5): Number of random seeds (noise realizations) to run per damping.
123:     \item \texttt{--n\_dampings} (Default: 8): Number of points in the damping grid.
124:     \item \texttt{--Q\_min} (Default: 0.05): Minimum quality factor of the sweep.
125:     \item \texttt{--Q\_max} (Default: 15.0): Maximum quality factor of the sweep.
126:     \item \texttt{--geometries} (Default: \texttt{square,triangular,honeycomb}): Geometries to sweep.
127:     \item \texttt{--R} (Default: 0.025): Circumscribed radius $R$ enclosing each needle.
128:     \item \texttt{--B\_max\_factor} (Default: 8.0): Peak external field $B_{\max}$ as a multiple of nearest-neighbor field $B_{\mathrm{ref}}$.
129:     \item \texttt{--t\_sim\_periods} (Default: 40.0): Total simulation cycle duration in natural units of $T_0 = 2\pi/\omega_0$.
130:     \item \texttt{--pbc} (Default: 0): Periodic boundary conditions (0 = off, 1 = on).
131:     \item \texttt{--use\_gpu} (Default: 0): Enable GPU acceleration (CuPy).
132:     \item \texttt{--skip\_relax\_stage} (Default: 0): If set to 1, skips the Stage 2 relaxation run.
133:     \item \texttt{--damping\_noise} (Default: 0.0): Relative per-needle damping noise variation.
134:     \item \texttt{--needle\_frac} (Default: 0.80): Needle length fraction of $2R$.
135:     \item \texttt{--needle\_thickness} (Default: 0.0004): Steel sheet thickness of the needles.
136:     \item \texttt{--steel\_density} (Default: 7850.0): Steel density [kg/m$^3$].
137:     \item \texttt{--steel\_Bsat} (Default: None): Steel saturation flux density [T].
138:     \item \texttt{--moment} (Default: None): Explicit override for magnetic moment [A$\cdot$m$^2$].
139:     \item \texttt{--inertia} (Default: None): Explicit override for moment of inertia [kg$\cdot$m$^2$].
140:     \item \texttt{--dt\_factor} (Default: 0.05): Time step $\Delta t$ as a fraction of $T_0$.
141:     \item \texttt{--noise} (Default: 1.5): Initial orientation noise amplitude [rad].
142:     \item \texttt{--domain\_tol} (Default: 15.0): Angular tolerance for domain clustering.
143:     \item \texttt{--quick\_test} (Default: 0): If set to 1, overrides grid size to 12, seeds to 1, dampings to 3, and duration to 8 periods for fast pipeline verification.
144: \end{itemize}
145: 
146: \subsection{Output Structure}
147: The script creates the following file hierarchy under \texttt{--out\_dir}:
148: \begin{lstlisting}
149: <out_dir>/
150: +- manifest.csv
151: +- hysteresis/
152: |  +- square_damp00_seed00.csv
153: |  +- ...
154: +- relax/
155: |  +- square_damp00_seed00.csv
156: |  +- ...
157: +- meta/
158:    +- square_damp00_seed00_hyst.json
159:    +- square_damp00_seed00_relax.json
160: \end{lstlisting}
161: 
162: % ======================================================================
163: \section{\texttt{damping\_sweepV02.py} (Parallel Campaign)}
164: \label{sec:damping_sweep_v02}
165: % ======================================================================
166: 
167: The parallelized version \texttt{damping\_sweepV02.py} introduces significant performance and reliability enhancements:
168: 
169: \subsection{Worker Process Sandboxing}
170: To prevent concurrency collisions, the simulator's writing of temporary files (such as \texttt{hysteresis\_loop.csv}) is isolated. The script utilizes Python's \texttt{ProcessPoolExecutor} to distribute runs across multiple cores. An initializer script maps each worker process to its own private scratch directory (\texttt{\_scratch/worker\_xxxx/}) inside the output tree.
171: \noindent\textbf{Note:} When running \texttt{damping\_sweepV02.py}, the output directory path \texttt{--out\_dir} should be supplied as an \textbf{absolute path} (e.g., using \texttt{"\$(pwd)/results"}), so that sandboxed workers can resolve and copy files back to the parent directory correctly.
172: 
173: \subsection{Incremental Manifest \& Resume Support}
174: Unlike the serial version which writes the manifest at the end, V02 updates \texttt{manifest.csv} incrementally after \textbf{each completed run} and flushes it immediately. 
175: If the campaign is aborted (due to power loss, user interrupt, etc.), it can be resumed by passing \texttt{--resume 1}. The script checks the existing manifest, identifies which jobs are already completed, and skips them automatically.
176: 
177: \subsection{CLI Additions in V02}
178: In addition to all the parameters of the serial version, V02 supports:
179: \begin{itemize}
180:     \item \texttt{--n\_workers} (Default: 1): Number of processes to run in parallel.
181:     \item \texttt{--resume} (Default: 0): Set to 1 to resume a previously interrupted run in the same directory.
182: \end{itemize}
183: 
184: % ======================================================================
185: \section{\texttt{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py} (Post-Processing)}
186: \label{sec:damping_analysis}
187: % ======================================================================
188: 
189: The script \texttt{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py} processes the data directory generated by either of the sweep scripts. 
190: 
191: \subsection{Hysteresis Metric Extraction}
192: For each Stage 1 hysteresis run, the script calculates:
193: \begin{itemize}
194:     \item \textbf{Loop Area}: Computed using Green's theorem to evaluate the closed-line integral $\oint M(B) dB$. It measures the energy dissipated per cycle.
195:     \item \textbf{Coercive Field} ($B_c$): Estimated by linear interpolation of the field $B$ when magnetization crosses zero ($M = 0$).
196:     \item \textbf{Remanent Magnetization} ($M_r$): Estimated by linear interpolation of the magnetization $M$ when the external field crosses zero ($B = 0$).
197: \end{itemize}
198: 
199: \subsection{Avalanche Event Detection}
200: Avalanches are discrete magnetic jumps where multiple needles flip collectively. The script detects them by:
201: \begin{enumerate}
202:     \item Isolating the monotonic field ramps (excluding regions where the field is constant).
203:     \item Computing the derivative of magnetization with respect to the field ($dM/dB$) for each step.
204:     \item Calculating a robust threshold using the Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) of the derivative:
205:     \begin{equation}
206:     \text{Threshold} = \text{Median}\left(\frac{dM}{dB}\right) + \kappa \cdot \text{MAD}\left(\frac{dM}{dB}\right)
207:     \end{equation}
208:     where $\kappa$ is configured via \texttt{--mad\_threshold} (default: 4.0).
209:     \item Integrating consecutive steps that exceed the threshold to define the avalanche size $s$.
210: \end{enumerate}
211: 
212: \subsection{Power-Law Exponent ($\tau$) Fitting via MLE}
213: The probability distribution of avalanche sizes in critical systems is expected to follow a power law: $P(s) \sim s^{-\tau}$ for $s \geq x_{\min}$. The script fits this distribution using Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE).
214: To ensure statistical robustness, the script **aggregates the avalanche sizes from all seeds** for each geometry and damping value before fitting.
215: The fit process is as follows:
216: \begin{enumerate}
217:     \item It iterates over candidate values for the lower bound $x_{\min}$ (quantiles of the sample).
218:     \item For each candidate $x_{\min}$, it computes the MLE estimator for the continuous power-law exponent:
219:     \begin{equation}
220:     \hat{\tau} = 1 + n \left[ \sum_{i=1}^n \ln\left(\frac{s_i}{x_{\min}}\right) \right]^{-1}
221:     \end{equation}
222:     \item It evaluates the Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) distance between the empirical data distribution and the theoretical power-law distribution.
223:     \item It selects the $x_{\min}$ candidate that minimizes the KS distance.
224:     \item Fits are discarded if the remaining tail has fewer than 15 data points (\texttt{n\_tail < 15}).
225: \end{enumerate}
226: 
227: \subsection{Output Files and Renders}
228: The analysis script saves:
229: \begin{itemize}
230:     \item \texttt{summary\_hysteresis.csv}: Loop metrics and power-law fit parameters per run.
231:     \item \texttt{aggregated\_loop\_metrics.csv}: Mean and standard deviation of loop area, $B_c$, and $M_r$ across seeds.
232:     \item \texttt{powerlaw\_fits.csv}: Detailed MLE power-law exponents $\tau$, standard errors, selected $x_{\min}$, and tail counts per geometry/damping.
233:     \item \texttt{area\_vs\_Q.png}, \texttt{Bc\_vs\_Q.png}, \texttt{Mr\_vs\_Q.png}: Loop metrics vs. quality factor curves.
234:     \item \texttt{tau\_vs\_Q.png}: Exponent $\tau$ vs. $Q$ curve (the central physics test of the study).
235:     \item \texttt{avalanche\_histograms.png}: Log-log plots of probability density $P(s)$ vs. avalanche size $s$ showing the data points and the fitted MLE line.
236:     \item \texttt{domain\_size\_vs\_Q.png}: Plots of the mean domain size and total number of domains after Stage 2 relaxation.
237: \end{itemize}
238: 
239: % ======================================================================
240: \section{Practical Recipes}
241: \label{sec:recipes}
242: % ======================================================================
243: 
244: \subsection{Quick Campaign Verification (CPU, parallel)}
245: To run a fast test sweep across 4 cores and analyze it immediately:
246: \begin{lstlisting}
247: # Run a quick test sweep
248: python3 damping_sweepV02.py --out_dir "$(pwd)/test_run" --quick_test 1 --n_workers 4
249: 
250: # Run the analysis
251: python3 damping_sweep_analysis.py --sweep_dir test_run --out_dir test_run/analysis
252: \end{lstlisting}
253: 
254: \subsection{Full Scientific Campaign (CPU, parallel)}
255: To run the full campaign with 8 dampings, 5 seeds, and 3 geometries (240 runs total) in parallel:
256: \begin{lstlisting}
257: # Run the campaign
258: python3 damping_sweepV02.py --out_dir "$(pwd)/results_campaign" --n_workers 8
259: 
260: # Analyze the campaign and generate final plots
261: python3 damping_sweep_analysis.py --sweep_dir results_campaign --out_dir results_campaign/analysis
262: \end{lstlisting}
263: 
264: \subsection{Resume an Interrupted Campaign}
265: If a campaign in \texttt{results\_campaign} is interrupted:
266: \begin{lstlisting}
267: python3 damping_sweepV02.py --out_dir "$(pwd)/results_campaign" --n_workers 8 --resume 1
268: \end{lstlisting}
