282: \subsection{Full campaign (ready to run)}
283: 
284: Defined parameters: $30\times30$ lattice (900 needles), 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\,15]$, 5 seeds, 3 geometries $\Rightarrow$ 120 hysteresis runs + 120 relaxation runs. Cost with V39 code: $\sim$2--2.5~min per run ($\approx 7$--8~h serial campaign). \textbf{With V40 optimization} (precomputed dipolar tensor, Section~\ref{sec:improvements}): $\sim$3.3--3.4~s per run $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{$\sim$15~min serial campaign on a single core}, and only a few minutes with \code{--n\_workers} on the \code{jps@mumax} server, with no GPU required.
285: 
286: % ======================================================================
287: \section{Step-by-step roadmap for the proposed studies}
288: \label{sec:roadmap}
289: % ======================================================================
290: 
291: This section details, in operational format, the four studies identified as publication candidates, from the central study to the satellites. For each study it specifies the question, simulation steps, analysis, expected result, and editorial destination. All use only already implemented capabilities (V39 + campaign scripts), except where indicated.
292: 
293: \subsection{Study 1 (central): damping as a control parameter for hysteresis and avalanches}
294: 
295: \textbf{Question.} Does the quality factor $Q$ control the universality class of avalanches and the shape of the hysteresis loop under complete dipolar coupling? Does the answer depend on geometry?
296: 
297: \begin{enumerate}
298: \item \textbf{Calibration} ($\sim$30~min): isolated needle in pulse mode; fit the free-decay envelope and validate that the measured $Q$ agrees with $Q = \omega_0 I / b$. This ensures that the damping grid actually spans both regimes;
299: \item \textbf{Hysteresis campaign}: $30\times30$ array; 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\,15]$; 5 seeds; 3 geometries; five-ramp cycle with $B_{\max} = 8\,B_{\mathrm{ref}}$ and duration $\propto T_0$ (120 runs, $\sim$4.5~h on serial CPU without V40);
300: \item \textbf{Relaxation campaign}: same combinations with $B = 0$, starting from the same noise, up to the $S$ plateau; domain statistics in the final state (120 runs, $\sim$3~h without V40);
301: \item \textbf{Analysis} (\code{damping\_sweep\_analysis.py}): area, $B_c$, and $M_r$ vs.\ $Q$ by geometry; $\tau(Q)$ by MLE with avalanches aggregated across seeds; domain size/number vs.\ $Q$;
302: \item \textbf{Robustness} (mandatory before conclusions): convergence in $\Delta t$ at the extremes of $Q$; sizes $N = 20$ and $40$ at $Q \approx 1$; comparison with/without PBC;
303: \item \textbf{Decision and writing}: A/B criterion from Section~\ref{sec:research}; framing as a ``model for \emph{artificial spin ice} / patterned nanomagnets'' in the introduction.
304: \end{enumerate}
305: 
306: \textbf{Expected result.} The pilot suggests increasing $\tau$ with $Q$ in the three geometries. If confirmed with full statistics and surviving robustness diagnostics, this is a type-B candidate result. \textbf{Destination:} ACS Materials Au (result A) or an attempt at PRL (result B), with ACS Physical Chemistry Au as backup.
307: 
308: \subsection{Study 2 (satellite): geometric frustration and domain-formation kinetics}
309: 
310: \textbf{Question.} Does geometric frustration (triangular $>$ honeycomb $>$ square) accelerate or slow down domain coarsening? Is the non-monotonicity of domain size with $Q$ seen in the triangular-lattice pilot real?
311: 
312: \begin{enumerate}
313: \item Long free relaxation ($B = 0$) in the three geometries, same seeds, for 3--4 representative $Q$ values;
314: \item Record $S(t)$ and label domains at intermediate times. This \emph{requires minor instrumentation}: domain statistics are currently computed only in the final state; periodic labeling \emph{snapshots} must be added along the trajectory;
315: \item Extract the time to the $S$ plateau and the growth law of the characteristic domain size $L(t) \sim t^{n}$, comparing exponent $n$ across geometries and damping regimes.
316: \end{enumerate}
317: 
318: \textbf{Destination:} may form part of the central article (domain section) or, if the frustration~$\times$~inertia interaction is rich, a separate article in ACS Physical Chemistry Au. Computational cost is low, since no field ramps are required.
319: 
320: \subsection{Study 3 (satellite): memory of the \emph{director} after a pulse}
321: 
322: \textbf{Question.} After a saturating field pulse, does the \emph{director} (emergent preferred direction, concept from the 1998 work~\cite{prb1998}) formed during relaxation retain memory of the pulse direction, or is it erased and redetermined by the initial noise? Equilibrium Monte Carlo cannot answer this, since it requires a real time trajectory.
323: 
324: \begin{enumerate}
325: \item Pulse mode with field direction $\varphi$ swept, for example from $0^\circ$ to $90^\circ$ in $15^\circ$ steps, several seeds, and 2--3 $Q$ values;
326: \item Measure the direction of the final \emph{director} (mean orientation of the main domains) as a function of $\varphi$;
327: \item Quantify the directional correlation $\langle \cos 2(\theta_{\mathrm{dir}} - \varphi) \rangle$ and its dependence on $Q$. The interesting hypothesis is that memory is stronger in the overdamped regime, without oscillations that ``shake'' the system out of the valley selected by the pulse.
328: \end{enumerate}
329: 
330: \textbf{Destination:} connects to the recent literature on \emph{clocked dynamics} and training in \emph{artificial spin ice}; a good candidate for a second article. Moderate cost.
