% XIII LAW3M 2026 abstract
% Prepared according to the abstract template: one page, Times 12 pt body, up to three references.
\documentclass[12pt,a4paper]{article}

\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{mathptmx}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{geometry}
\usepackage{setspace}
\usepackage[normalem]{ulem}
\usepackage{microtype}
\geometry{top=2.0cm,bottom=2.0cm,left=2.2cm,right=2.2cm}
\pagestyle{empty}
\setlength{\parindent}{0pt}
\setlength{\parskip}{6pt}
\singlespacing

\begin{document}

\begin{center}
{\bfseries\fontsize{14}{16}\selectfont
Inertia-controlled universality of magnetic avalanches\\
in classical dipolar needle arrays}\par
\vspace{10pt}
{\bfseries\fontsize{12}{14}\selectfont \uline{Jo\~ao~P.~Sinnecker}}\par
\vspace{4pt}
{\itshape\fontsize{11}{13}\selectfont
Centro Brasileiro de Pesquisas F\'isicas (CBPF), Rio de Janeiro, Brazil}
\end{center}
\vspace{8pt}

We report simulations of two-dimensional arrays of rotating magnetic compass needles
interacting through the full dipolar coupling tensor, with real Newtonian inertial dynamics.
Each needle obeys the rotational equation of motion
$I\ddot{\theta}_{i} = \tau_{i}^{\rm dip} + \tau_{i}^{\rm ext} - b\dot{\theta}_{i}$,
where $I$ is the moment of inertia and $b$ the viscous damping coefficient.
The dimensionless quality factor $Q = \omega_0 I/b$, where
$\omega_0 = \sqrt{mB_{\rm ref}/I}$ is the natural precession frequency set by the
dipolar coupling field between nearest neighbours $B_{\rm ref}$,
spans three regimes: underdamped ($Q \gg 1$, inertial overshoot and
chain-reaction avalanches), critically damped ($Q \approx 1$), and overdamped
($Q \ll 1$, monotonic relaxation and narrow loops).

We address the question: \textit{does the damping regime control the universality
class of magnetic avalanches, and does the answer depend on lattice geometry?}
Campaigns are performed on $30\times30$ arrays in square, triangular, and honeycomb
geometries, sweeping $Q$ over two decades
($Q \in [0.02,\,30]$, 14 log-spaced values, 8 random seeds per point).
Five-ramp hysteresis cycles are applied with a peak field
$B_{\max} = 8\,B_{\rm ref}$, chosen to ensure saturation at all $Q$.
Avalanche sizes are extracted from the direct spin-flip counter recorded at each
integration step and fitted by maximum-likelihood estimation to power-law distributions
following Clauset, Shalizi \& Newman [1], with a log-normal likelihood-ratio test
to validate the power-law hypothesis.
Waiting-time distributions between successive avalanches distinguish Poisson
(uncorrelated) from scale-free (SOC-like) inter-event timing.
The temporal autocorrelation time of $M(t)$ is tracked as an observable proxy
for inertia-driven memory, extending recent results on echo-state capacity in
artificial spin ice [2].

A pilot study (196 needles, 5 $Q$ values, 3 seeds) already reveals the central
signal: the avalanche exponent $\tau$ increases systematically with $Q$
in all three geometries, from $\tau \approx 1.2$--$1.3$ near the crossover
($Q \approx 0.87$) to $\tau \approx 1.7$--$1.8$ in the underdamped limit
($Q = 15$), with the triangular lattice showing non-monotonic domain-size
behaviour as a function of $Q$ absent from the other geometries.
The hysteresis loop area and coercive field grow monotonically as the system
moves into the overdamped regime, reflecting increased dissipation.
The full production campaign and implications for classifying nanomagnetic
arrays through their avalanche statistics are discussed in terms of the gap
identified in Ref.~[3], namely that no study has yet tested whether inertia
and dipolar anisotropy jointly modify avalanche universality classes.

\vspace{4pt}
{\small
[1] A.~Clauset, C.~R.~Shalizi and M.~E.~J.~Newman, SIAM Rev.\ \textbf{51}, 661 (2009).\par
[2] R.~Taniguchi, Sci.\ Rep.\ \textbf{15}, 12345 (2025).\par
[3] K.~A.~Dahmen, Y.~Ben-Zion and J.~T.~Uhl, Nat.\ Phys.\ \textbf{7}, 554 (2011).}

\end{document}

