{\itshape\fontsize{12}{14}\selectfont Centro Brasileiro de Pesquisas F\'isicas (CBPF), Rio de Janeiro, Brazil}\par
\end{center}
\vspace{10pt}

Artificial spin-ice arrays of lithographically patterned magnetic nanoislands provide a direct route to study how local dipolar coupling produces collective magnetic states~[1]. However, in nanofabricated systems it is difficult to tune the magnetic damping independently: the effective Gilbert damping $\alpha$ is a material property and switching barriers arise from shape anisotropy and fabrication disorder, not from controllable parameters. One may ask, therefore, why not simulate monodomain nanoisland arrays directly instead of compass needles. The answer is that a compass needle obeying Newton's second law for rotation, $I\ddot{\theta}_{i} = \tau_{i}^{\rm dip} + \tau_{i}^{\rm ext} - b\dot{\theta}_{i}$, provides something nanoisland models cannot: a single, physically meaningful, continuously tunable control parameter --- the quality factor $Q = \omega_0 I/b$, where $\omega_0 = \sqrt{mB_{\rm ref}/I}$ is the natural precession frequency set by nearest-neighbour dipolar coupling --- that spans from the overdamped limit ($Q \ll 1$, slow monotonic reversal, narrow loops) to the underdamped limit ($Q \gg 1$, inertial overshoot and chain-reaction avalanche cascades), with the critical-damping crossover at $Q \approx 1$ accessible by construction. Here we use this model to address a question that remains open in the spin-ice literature~[2]: does the dynamical regime set by damping control the universality class of magnetic avalanches, and does the answer depend on lattice geometry? We perform systematic campaigns on $30\times30$ square, triangular, and honeycomb arrays sweeping $Q$ over two decades, applying five-ramp hysteresis cycles and extracting avalanche statistics via maximum-likelihood power-law fitting~[3]. The three geometries provide controlled examples of increasing dipolar frustration, and they serve as macroscopic analogues of the square, kagome, and brick-wall artificial spin-ice geometries studied experimentally. Avalanche sizes are resolved both from the magnetisation derivative and from the direct spin-flip counter at each integration step, and the waiting-time distribution between successive avalanches discriminates between Poisson and scale-free inter-event timing. A pilot study already reveals the central signal: the power-law exponent $\tau$ increases systematically with $Q$ across all three geometries, from $\tau \approx 1.2$--$1.3$ near the crossover to $\tau \approx 1.7$--$1.8$ in the underdamped limit, while the hysteresis loop area and coercive field grow monotonically as the system becomes overdamped. The triangular lattice additionally shows non-monotonic domain-size behaviour with $Q$ absent from the other geometries, a candidate signature of frustration-inertia coupling. These results suggest that inertial dynamics, often neglected in spin-ice models, is a relevant variable for classifying the avalanche universality of dipolar arrays.
\vspace{6pt}

[1] S.~H.~Skj{\ae}rv{\o} \textit{et al.}, Nat. Rev. Phys. \textbf{2}, 13 (2020).\par
[2] K.~A.~Dahmen, Y.~Ben-Zion and J.~T.~Uhl, Nat. Phys. \textbf{7}, 554 (2011).\par
[3] A.~Clauset, C.~R.~Shalizi and M.~E.~J.~Newman, SIAM Rev. \textbf{51}, 661 (2009).

